---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { checks, periods, quarterLabel, sources } from "../lib/ledger";

const src = sources().sort((a, b) => a.dataset.localeCompare(b.dataset) || a.original_name.localeCompare(b.original_name));
const byDataset = Object.entries(Object.groupBy(src, (s) => s.dataset));
const DATASET: Record<string, string> = {
  "uscis-forms": "USCIS · Number of Service-wide Forms by Quarter, Form Status, and Processing Time",
  "uscis-i130-awaiting": "USCIS · Form I-130 Approved Family-Based Petitions Awaiting Visa Availability",
  "state-iv": "U.S. Department of State · Monthly Immigrant Visa Issuances by Foreign State of Chargeability",
};
const nChecks = checks().length;
const wayback = src.filter((s) => s.retrieved_by === "wayback").length;
const firstQ = (() => { const p = periods("total", "received")[0]; return `${quarterLabel(p)} (${p})`; })();
---
<Base title="Methodology" description="How the USA Immigration Ledger turns USCIS and State Department spreadsheets into one verifiable dataset: sources, definitions, rules, limits and corrections.">
  <p class="kicker">How we did it</p>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="dek">The short version: we archive every government file byte for byte, parse each cell without guessing, and refuse to publish a number we can't trace to a cell.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>USCIS</strong>, the agency in the Department of Homeland Security that decides immigration benefits, publishes quarterly statistics at
      {" "}<a href="https://www.uscis.gov/tools/reports-and-studies/immigration-and-citizenship-data">uscis.gov/tools/reports-and-studies/immigration-and-citizenship-data</a>.
      A crawler downloads new files from that page once a week, identifying itself and pausing between requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The State Department</strong> issues immigrant visas at consulates abroad. Its monthly issuance files are at
      {" "}<a href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-statistics/immigrant-visa-statistics/monthly-immigrant-visa-issuances.html">travel.state.gov</a>.
      That site refuses automated downloads, and we don't work around that. Its files are downloaded by a person and
      added by hand, so they can lag. These are visas issued abroad, a different step from USCIS decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each file is stored in the <a href="https://github.com/sigmalgebra/usa-immigration-ledger/tree/main/archive">public archive</a>
      under its SHA-256 hash, because USCIS removes old versions from its site. The build checks every file against its hash.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deleted reports.</strong> Both agencies remove older files. {wayback} of our {src.length} files are copies
      the <a href="https://web.archive.org/">Internet Archive</a> captured before they disappeared. For each one we
      record the original government address and the exact capture we downloaded, in its raw form (the archive's
      <code>id_</code> address, which returns the original bytes). That is how the series reaches back to
      {" "}{firstQ}.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>PDF-only years.</strong> USCIS's reports for FY2019Q3 to FY2021Q3, and the State Department's for March
      2017 to September 2021, survive only as PDFs. Table-extraction tools misread them, merging form numbers and
      splitting digits, so we read each number from its position on the page, under its column header. Citations for
      PDF values give the page, the column, and the distance from the top of the page in points. Two checks back the
      method: each State Department month's grand total equals the sum of its rows, and USCIS PDF values agree with
      the neighbouring reports that reprint the same quarters about as often as spreadsheets agree with each other.
      Both are automated tests.
    </p>

    <h2>Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Coverage.</strong> USCIS forms from {firstQ}. The FY2012Q3–FY2013Q3 files are a different, monthly report (receipts and approvals only); we archive them but start the series where the full quarterly report begins.</li>
      <li><strong>Fiscal quarters.</strong> The federal fiscal year starts October 1. FY2026Q3 is April 1 to June 30, 2026.</li>
      <li><strong>Received</strong>: new filings entered into a case system in the quarter. <strong>Approved</strong>, <strong>denied</strong> and <strong>completed</strong>: decisions made in the quarter, which may be on cases filed earlier. <strong>Pending</strong>: cases awaiting a decision on the quarter's last day.</li>
      <li><strong>Median processing time</strong>: USCIS's figure for cases decided in the quarter, in months from receipt to completion.</li>
      <li><strong>Total</strong> is USCIS's own service-wide line. It includes forms not listed individually, so it is larger than the sum of the rows, and its scope has changed over the years.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rules we follow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>No guessing.</strong> Any cell we can't parse, or row we can't identify, stops the build.</li>
      <li><strong>Identity is reviewed by a person.</strong> Which printed rows count as the same form across years is decided in one reviewed file, <a href="https://github.com/sigmalgebra/usa-immigration-ledger/blob/main/catalog/uscis-forms.toml">catalog/uscis-forms.toml</a>, with a note wherever USCIS split, merged or renamed a row. We never join series across a split.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing stays missing.</strong> A dash, “N/A” or a privacy “D” is published with that status, not as zero. USCIS's table key says a dash means “zero or rounds to 0”; you can treat it that way if you choose.</li>
      <li><strong>No interpolation.</strong> Quarters USCIS doesn't publish are gaps.</li>
      <li><strong>Latest printing wins, and history is kept.</strong> If a later file restates an earlier number, we show the later value and keep both in <a href="/data/v1/revisions.csv">revisions.csv</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Doubts are published.</strong> {nChecks} automated checks run on every build and are shown on <a href="/source-errors/">Errors we found</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Prose can't drift from data.</strong> Every number in our text is drawn from the dataset when the site is built; a number that isn't in the data fails the build.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="questions">Open questions for USCIS and the State Department</h2>
    <p>Before relying on these findings, we are putting these questions to the agencies. Their answers, or the lack of one, will be logged here with dates.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>USCIS: what explains the fall in completions from early 2024 to 2026, and in naturalization decisions since mid-2025?</li>
      <li>USCIS: why do the FY2024Q3 processing times for dozens of forms repeat FY2024Q2's values digit for digit? Which values are correct?</li>
      <li>USCIS: the FY2024Q2 and FY2026Q2 I-130 visa-availability files are titled with earlier dates. Can you confirm their correct dates?</li>
      <li>USCIS: what produced the fractional counts in the FY2024 reports, and how were they estimated?</li>
      <li>State Department: why do some Egypt and Jordan immediate-relative visa classes appear on two rows in several 2023–2024 months?</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Status: not yet sent. This page will show when each question was sent and answered.</p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>No corrections yet. Found an error? <a href="https://github.com/sigmalgebra/usa-immigration-ledger/issues/new">Open an issue</a>; corrections are logged here with the date and what changed.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Every source file</h2>
  {byDataset.map(([ds, files]) => (
    <details>
      <summary><strong>{DATASET[ds] ?? ds}</strong> ({files!.length} files)</summary>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th><th>Retrieved</th><th>Original</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {files!.map((f) => (
              <tr>
                <td><a href={f.archive_url}>{f.original_name}</a></td>
                <td><code title={f.sha256}>{f.sha256.slice(0, 12)}…</code></td>
                <td>{f.retrieved_at.slice(0, 10)} ({f.retrieved_by})</td>
                <td>{f.via ? <><a href={f.via}>Internet Archive copy</a> of <a href={f.url}>{new URL(f.url).hostname}</a></> : f.url.includes("#") ? "downloaded by hand" : <a href={f.url}>{new URL(f.url).hostname}</a>}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  ))}
</Base>
